Potassium chloride $\left({ }^{39} \mathrm{~K}^{35} \mathrm{Cl}\right)$ is a colorless and transparent ionic crystal with a density of $\rho=2.0 \mathrm{~g} / \mathrm{cm}^{3}$;
(1) Calculate the nearest neighbor distance between $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ in this solid;
(2) Derive the lower limit of the energy gap between the valence band and the conduction band based on the given optical properties;
(3) Provide the relationship between the specific heat $c$ of solid KCl and temperature $T$;
(4) Briefly discuss the magnetic properties of this solid (such as paramagnetism, diamagnetism, ferromagnetism, etc.).

An atom has four lowest energy levels:

\[ E_{0}=-14.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{1}=-9.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{2}=-7.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{3}=-5.5 \, \mathrm{eV} \]

The transition rate \(A_{ij}\) (Einstein coefficient) for the transition \(i \rightarrow j\) is

\[
\begin{aligned}
& A_{20} = 1.2 \times 10^{8} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}, \quad A_{21} = 8.0 \times 10^{7} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
\]

Calculate the radiative lifetime of the \(E_{2}\) energy level.

A thin ring with 500 turns of wire and an average circumference of 50 cm carries a current of 0.3 A, and the relative permeability of the iron core is 600.  
(1) The magnetic flux density $B$ in the iron core is $\qquad$;  
(2) The magnetic field strength $H$ in the iron core is $\qquad$ $\left(\mu_{0}=4 \pi \times 10^{-7} \mathrm{~T} \cdot \mathrm{~m} \cdot \mathrm{~A}^{-1}\right)$.

According to quantum mechanics, the distribution of the charge outside the nucleus of a hydrogen atom in its normal state is as follows: at a distance $r$ from the core, the volume charge density is given by $\rho(r)=-q \mathrm{e}^{-2 r / a} /\left(\pi a^{3}\right)$, where $q=1.60 \times 10^{-19} \mathrm{C}$ is the absolute value of the total charge outside the nucleus, and $a=5.29 \times 10^{-11} \mathrm{~m}$ is the Bohr radius. Find:
(1) The total charge outside the nucleus;
(2) The electric field intensity $\boldsymbol{E}$ at a distance $r$ from the nucleus.

A particle with mass \( m \) is undergoing forced oscillation under the combined action of the restoring force \(-kx\), the damping force \(-\gamma \dot{x}\), and a periodic driving force

\[
F(t) = 
\begin{cases} 
A \sin \omega t & 0 < t \leq \frac{\pi}{\omega} \\ 
0 & \frac{\pi}{\omega} < t \leq \frac{2\pi}{\omega} 
\end{cases}
\]

Solve for the third term \( a_2 \) in the Fourier series expansion of \( f(t) \) in the steady-state equation of motion using the first four terms.

The equation of state and the specific heat capacity at constant volume of an interacting atomic gas are given by the following expressions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
p(T, V) &= a T^{1/2} + b T^{3} + c V^{-2}, \\
c_{\mathrm{v}}(T, V) &= d T^{1/2} V + e T^{2} V + f T^{1/2}
\end{aligned}
$$

where $a \sim f$ are constants independent of $T$ and $V$.  
(2) Determine the relationships among the constants $a \sim f$ based on the fact that $U(T,V)$ is a state function.
